Patch 7934 (experiment, GPU time): VPULL_VRAM=1 lets the GPU read the batch's pulled vertex bytes and indices from video memory.

Why. VPULL's byte ring and the index buffer are UPLOAD heaps (system memory): every vertex-shader load of a pulled vertex and every
index fetch crosses PCIe. The test10 desktop log pulls 100 % of its indexed draws and uploads 7.1-7.9 GB of vertex bytes per 5 s in
fights (60-80 MB per batch at 3200-4000 draws), the first suspect for its 50-70 ms GPU frames.
What. With the switch, two DEFAULT-heap mirrors are made at init (ring size + slack, and the index half: about 144 MB of video memory
with the default 128 MB ring). The start of each batch's draw loop copies the used span of the batch's ring half (+ the Load2 slack)
and of its index half into them (barriers COMMON -> COPY_DEST -> NON_PIXEL_SHADER_RESOURCE / INDEX_BUFFER; buffers decay to COMMON
between ExecuteCommandLists, and batches run in queue order, so one mirror serves every batch, also with GPU_OVERLAP=1). The copy
span is the batch's high-water mark of the ring offset (a guard; the offset only grows today). The draws
bind the mirrors at the same offsets. The CPU writes exactly as before. [VPULL_VRAM] every 600 batches: MB copied per batch;
GPU_TIME=1 shows the copy as "vram-copy". Mirror allocation fails: the old binding. Off by default.

